    18	
    19	Eight had come down that road at a run, and had stopped in the air.
    20	
    21	He had made the factor say it twice. The old man with the stick had stepped out, and struck the ice once, and there had been a thing across the cut from one bank to the other, as high as the scree. Eight hounds broke on it. It stood for exactly as long as the man never lifted a foot; and when it went, he went down with it, like a tent with the pole drawn.
    22	
    23	Teague had never seen an Iron Wall. He had heard of them, as a man who works the holds hears of what is kept behind the walls of towns. They belonged to tribunals, and a person did not come by one in a meadow. Whoever carried such a thing up a border road on a stick had been somebody, a long way from here, and had left off being it; and Teague was not such a fool as to ask what.
    24	
