    90	
    91	There were ten on the count, and neither healer; a healer who is counted is a healer who is sent. Whatever the thing paid was to be shared by that count, and so was whatever it cost. The field was his. When it came to steel on a road there would be one voice, and he had heard the boy admit, with his feet, in a square of snow, whose voice it ought to be. And the reading was the pale one's. He could read a slip, and a ledger, and a man's hands; he could not read a calfskin with four rings on it, and he would not stand at her shoulder pretending.
    92	
    93	There was one thing more, and it was the name.
    94	
    95	He had seen two crews lie up together for a cold term and come out of it as one. He had seen them bury each other's dead as kin. It was a fine thing, and it was how a captain came to turn back for a man who was not his, on a ledge, with his own people strung out behind him. He had four, and he meant to walk four home. He would call the thing by the smallest word that would hold it, and he would not let anybody make it larger.
