   321	## 8. Major fights and set pieces: planning allowances (private staging the author owns)
   322	
   323	- **The cut (M3).** A hundred strides of road cut to a cart's width, with scree on both sides and a frozen runnel a stride wide down the middle. A sink lies fifty-five yards below [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 6] on the north side, iced and in shadow; there are eight hounds. Positions: Brom on stone at the south edge with a rock; Vastin a stride behind him; Lira on the south scree; Seln on the north scree, unseen; Cael twenty strides west, past the sink; Karis, Oryn and the mule at the east end. The decisions: the wall goes up and everything turns to the loudest thing; Brom and Lira work its south end silently; Cael bursts **away**, then goes still on the scree as the lock beat lands; the pack returns to the wall and breaks on it; the heap; the runnel. The opponent learns too: the den has learned the road. Costs to carry: Brom's foot; Vastin's knee (a month); Cael's burst bill.
   324	- **The alley (M5).** Twenty-two steps in five flights, a stride and a half wide, walled three men high with no doors. There are four at the top (a crossbow and a signaller on the landing) and two coming up from the yard gate with staves. The plane sits at the second flight's top step, wall to wall; Cael stands two feet below it. Brom has a loose step-stone. Lira drops from the second flight. Seln's cord comes from the roof onto the signaller's arm and the rail. It lasts "a minute and some". The opponent's plan was built for a declaration and meets a wall and a rock. Costs: Karis's hand; Vastin past his limit; Brom's good shoulder used wrong.
   325	- **The Span (M8).** A shelf a cart's width wide, with the sink's three-man drop on the left and the iron on the right; the line lies a stride past the iron. A barricade of two timbers stands at the shelf's narrowest; past the fire is a pinch a stride and a half wide, then a perimeter road a pace wide. There are seven defenders: two crossbows and an empty-handed low practitioner. Exchange one belongs to the watch. Exchange two: Teague's line, with Cael at his left a stride back. Exchange three: width (five inside two strides in, four on the shelf; walk; eleven seconds). Then the fall. **Fairness is entered before the act:** nothing is gained inside.
   326	- **The pass (M9).** A dry defile a hundred strides long with a loose-stone stream-bed. The west wall is scree to a shelf two-thirds up, carrying two timber-braced stacks, one with a roped dead pine; the east wall is sheer. The wall is a lean-to slant, high edge at the west wall ahead, low edge at the mouth behind. There are seven numbered volleys: Brom's forearm (1); the wall up (2); the fall (4); the broad man (5); the young one (6); Seln over the lip (7). Cael's push takes the second stack along the shelf, not off it. Teague goes up the stair left-handed; the tall man [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: M1 audit; OWNER-pending #59] reverses her spear. The team breaks along the ridge. **Win sentence:** Seln removed the team's ability to hold the high ground by climbing the slope its own rig had swept clean, and the wall bought him the climb.
   327	- **New set pieces** (shale-back fence; ice road; silent yard; Lira's bout; ferry landing; the tail; the seep). Each develops a problem the reader can follow: terrain, positions, objective, limit, a change of decision, and a cost carried forward. **Never put a Path or declaration on display that a sequence lock forbids** (§13).
